# Committed wasmtime readings for wait-forever.wasm (#1303, ADR 0128).
# Built:       wasm-tools parse wait-forever.wat -o wait-forever.wasm   (wasm-tools 1.258.0; validates --features all)
# Invocation:  timeout 2 wasmtime run -W threads=y,shared-memory=y --invoke <export> wait-forever.wasm
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime, darwin/arm64.
# Runs:        10 of 10 per line, each identical, taken 2026-11-21.
#
# Each line is `<export>: <wasmtime's reading>`, and the tests read them by export. `hangs` is: still running at the
# 2 s bound, ended there by timeout(1)'s SIGTERM (exit 124), nothing on stdout or stderr, and idle while it waited
# (0.00 s user and system time over the bound, read with /usr/bin/time). A number is what wasmtime printed on
# stdout, with exit 0. Its only stderr is the warning that `--invoke` of a function returning values is
# experimental.
forever: hangs
forever64: hangs
brief: 2
#
# Meaning: an infinite wait with no other agent to notify it is suspended for ever, and the 10 ms wait times out (2).
